<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malabar Coast</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Malabar Coast is the south-western coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> and neighbouring areas, long famous for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and spice trade. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a> on this coast in 1498, opening the sea route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to India. The coast became the first focus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>'s commercial and military efforts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malabar-coast.jpg" alt="Malabar Coast" width="320" height="362" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: w:Planemad · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:India_Malabar_Coast_locator_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The forests yielded sandalwood, which became a valuable royal monopoly, and the conquest of <b>Malabar</b> in 1766 brought pepper, cardamom and the ports of the west coast.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Cape of Good Hope in 1488, demonstrating the viability of an oceanic route to Asia, and under Manuel I, Vasco da Gama completed that route in 1498, reaching Calicut on the <b>Malabar Coast</b> and returning to Lisbon with a cargo reputedly worth many times the expedition’s cost.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Much natural knowledge also came from beyond Europe through trade and empire: the Hortus Malabaricus, a twelve-volume Dutch flora of the <b>Malabar Coast</b> published in Amsterdam from 1678, rested on the knowledge of the Malayali physician Itty Achudan and other local experts.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Malabar</b> posts were organised as a commandment governed from Fort Kochi, with factories at Cannanore, Cranganore and Quilon.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-malabar">Dutch Malabar</a></li><li><blockquote>Their territory covered the <b>Malabar</b> coast and the Kongu country inland; their capital Vanchi is identified with Karur in Tamil Nadu by most historians.</blockquote><a href="/state/early-cheras">Early Cheras</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 12th century the Mushika realm became the kingdom of Kolathunadu under the Kolathiri rajas, who ruled northern <b>Malabar</b> until the 18th century and met Vasco da Gama's successors.</blockquote><a href="/state/ezhimala-kingdom">Ezhimala kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
